WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.534
InsureCast forecasts monthly
insurance claims, and the cost per claim.

2
00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:08.542
Reserve planning needs
a forward view of claims and cost.

3
00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:13.515
Spreadsheets and one-off models are hard
to reproduce, segment and stress-test.

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:15.461
Pick a segment.

5
00:00:15.461 --> 00:00:18.791
Forecast up to 12 months,
with a 95% range.

6
00:00:18.791 --> 00:00:20.262
Then test what-ifs.

7
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.254
A segment is one state,
one industry and one claim type.

8
00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:30.952
Here: Texas, construction,
lost-time claims.

9
00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:35.696
Update, and both charts
refit to the new segment.

10
00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.269
A conclusion follows,
written from the same numbers.

11
00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:44.472
Gray is the history.

12
00:00:44.472 --> 00:00:46.832
Blue dashes are the 12-month forecast.

13
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:49.908
The shaded band is the 95% range.

14
00:00:49.908 --> 00:00:51.726
It widens further out.

15
00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.541
Cost per claim is forecast the same way.

16
00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.742
Now a what-if: cost per claim up 10%,
claim counts up 15%.

17
00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:06.009
Update, and both charts lift.

18
00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:12.628
December's cost per claim rises
from about $22,000 to $24,000.

19
00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:15.859
Every month in the table carries
the what-if.

20
00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:20.247
One forecast feeds the charts,
the table and the conclusion.

21
00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.021
InsureCast.

22
00:01:22.021 --> 00:01:25.460
Forecast claims and cost per claim,
then test the what-ifs.
